# The dodecahedral honeycomb {5, 3, 4}, the dodecagrid, hyperbolic three-space H3 tiled by right-angled regular
# dodecahedra, four of them meeting around each edge, modeled and proved by the kernel. This is Margenstern's
# three-dimensional analogue of the pentagrid {5, 4}: where the pentagrid packs the hyperbolic plane with right-angled
# pentagons (four at a vertex), the dodecagrid packs hyperbolic 3-space with right-angled dodecahedra (four around an
# edge). The Schlafli symbol reads outward by dimension. The first two entries {5, 3} name the CELL: a regular
# dodecahedron, twelve pentagonal faces, three faces meeting at each of its twenty vertices. The final 4 names how many
# such cells wrap around each edge of the honeycomb.
#
# The cell {5, 3} is a Platonic solid, SPHERICAL by the Schlafli test: the polygon-vertex defect (5 - 2)(3 - 2) = 3 is
# below 4, so the dodecahedron closes up on the sphere. A flat Euclidean dodecahedron has a dihedral angle near one
# hundred sixteen degrees, so three would leave a gap and four would overlap. In hyperbolic space the cell is GROWN until
# its dihedral angle is made exactly ninety degrees, a right angle, and then four cells fit perfectly around an edge
# because four right angles complete the full turn, 4 times 90 equals 360. That right-angle condition is what forces the
# honeycomb into H3, just as the right-angled pentagon forces the pentagrid into H2.
#
# This file builds the combinatorial structure of the cell and the local patch of the honeycomb. It constructs the
# dodecahedron's face, vertex, and edge counts, proves the cell's Euler characteristic is two (a genuine sphere, a closed
# polyhedron), proves the cell is spherical and the honeycomb hyperbolic via the Schlafli defect, names the four cells
# around an edge as an order-four cycle and proves the cycle is the identity (the final 4 of the symbol), checks the
# right-angle condition arithmetically, and proves a local incidence fact (three faces at every dodecahedron vertex) over
# a constructed patch. Two layers are checked: the arithmetic layer (Euler, defect, angle) with the kernel's built-in
# integer arithmetic, and the structural layer (the edge cycle and the vertex patch) with `fold`.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# THE CELL. The twelve pentagonal faces of the regular dodecahedron, named so the structural proofs can range over the
# faces of one cell. A dodecahedron has twelve faces, so this enum has exactly twelve cases.
form dodecahedron-face
  case face-one
  case face-two
  case face-three
  case face-four
  case face-five
  case face-six
  case face-seven
  case face-eight
  case face-nine
  case face-ten
  case face-eleven
  case face-twelve

# The face, vertex, and edge counts of the dodecahedron cell {5, 3}, the raw combinatorial data of the Platonic solid.
# Twelve pentagonal faces, twenty vertices, thirty edges. These feed the Euler characteristic below.

# The cell has twelve pentagonal faces, the 5 and the count together being the {5, ...} of the symbol.
task face-count
  like integer
  send back
    code 12

# The cell has twenty vertices.
task vertex-count
  like integer
  send back
    code 20

# The cell has thirty edges.
task edge-count
  like integer
  send back
    code 30

# Each face of the dodecahedron is a pentagon, five sides, the first entry of {5, 3, 4}.
task sides-per-face
  like integer
  send back
    code 5

# Three faces meet at each vertex of the dodecahedron, the second entry of {5, 3, 4}, the {q} of the cell.
task faces-per-vertex
  like integer
  send back
    code 3

# Four cells wrap around each edge of the honeycomb, the final entry of {5, 3, 4}.
task cells-around-edge
  like integer
  send back
    code 4

# THE LOCAL PATCH AROUND AN EDGE. Four dodecahedra meet around each edge of the honeycomb, the final 4 of the symbol. We
# name the four cells as a small enum, the local patch of the bulk, so the four-fold meeting is a constructed structure
# rather than a bare numeral.
form edge-cell
  case first
  case second
  case third
  case fourth

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition serves the edge cycle
# and the vertex face-incidence map, so each table reads cleanly as its rows.
tree map-cell
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# The cyclic order of the four cells around an edge: going around the edge visits first, second, third, fourth, and back
# to first. This is the order-four rotation that the four-around-an-edge fact means geometrically, the same shape as the
# four-around-a-ridge cycle of the 24-cell honeycomb one dimension up.
task around-edge
  take c, like edge-cell
  like edge-cell
  fork case, read c
    fuse map-cell, first, second
    fuse map-cell, second, third
    fuse map-cell, third, fourth
    fuse map-cell, fourth, first

# THE VERTEX PATCH. Three faces meet at each vertex of the dodecahedron, the {3} of the cell. We name the three faces
# around one vertex as a small enum and a cyclic order over them, so the three-fold incidence is a constructed local
# patch and the order-three cycle witnesses it.
form vertex-face
  case left
  case right
  case base

# The cyclic order of the three faces around a dodecahedron vertex: left, right, base, and back to left. An order-three
# rotation, the three faces meeting at the vertex.
task around-vertex
  take f, like vertex-face
  like vertex-face
  fork case, read f
    fuse map-cell, left, right
    fuse map-cell, right, base
    fuse map-cell, base, left

# THE EULER CHARACTERISTIC OF THE CELL IS TWO: vertices minus edges plus faces equals 20 - 30 + 12 = 2. A characteristic
# of two is the signature of a sphere, so the dodecahedron cell is a genuine closed polyhedron, a topological sphere with
# no handles. Computed with the kernel's built-in integer arithmetic from the constructed counts.
hold cell-euler-characteristic-is-two
  call is-equal
    call add
      call subtract
        code 20
        code 30
      code 12
    code 2

# THE CELL {5, 3} IS SPHERICAL: the polygon-vertex defect (5 - 2)(3 - 2) = 3 is below 4, the Schlafli test for a finite
# spherical tiling. The dodecahedron is one of the five Platonic solids, and this is why it closes up on the sphere
# rather than spreading across a plane. Ties to the dodecahedron case of the tessellation classifier.
hold cell-is-spherical
  call is-below
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 5
        code 2
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 2
    code 4

# THE HONEYCOMB {5, 3, 4} IS HYPERBOLIC. The cell is spherical, yet the honeycomb is hyperbolic, because the curvature
# enters one dimension higher: four cells meet around an edge only after the dihedral angle is forced to a right angle,
# which can happen only in hyperbolic space. The honeycomb test compares the cell's spherical angle data against the
# four-around-an-edge requirement. The dodecahedron's natural (Euclidean) dihedral angle exceeds ninety degrees, so four
# unmodified cells overrun the full turn around an edge: four copies of the natural angle would exceed 360. The honeycomb
# is hyperbolic precisely because this overrun must be absorbed by negative curvature. We witness the overrun as the
# strict inequality 4 times 116 is above 360 (using 116 degrees, the rounded natural dihedral angle of the dodecahedron).
hold honeycomb-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 116
    code 360

# THE RIGHT-ANGLE CONDITION. In hyperbolic space the cell is grown until its dihedral angle is exactly ninety degrees,
# and then four cells fit perfectly around an edge: four right angles complete the full turn, 4 times 90 equals 360. This
# is the exact closure that makes the dodecagrid a tiling, the three-dimensional echo of the right-angled pentagon's four
# at a vertex. Computed with integer arithmetic from the right angle and the cell count.
hold four-right-angles-close-the-turn
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 90
    code 360

# THE FOUR-AROUND-AN-EDGE COUNT IS FOUR: the dodecagrid has four cells around each edge, the count that distinguishes the
# honeycomb {5, 3, 4} from the lone Platonic dodecahedron {5, 3} and makes it a tiling of hyperbolic three-space. Stated
# as the constructed numeral.
hold cells-around-edge-is-four
  call is-equal
    call cells-around-edge
    code 4

# FOUR CELLS GO AROUND AN EDGE: applying the around-edge step four times returns to the start, around-edge four times is
# the identity, for each of the four cells. This is the four-fold meeting of dodecahedra at every edge, the final 4 of
# the Schlafli symbol {5, 3, 4}, proved as the order-four cycle of the local patch. Proved over all four cells by `fold`.
rule four-cells-around-an-edge
  seat c, like edge-cell
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call around-edge
        call around-edge
          call around-edge
            call around-edge
              read c
      read c
  fold c

# THREE FACES MEET AT EVERY VERTEX: applying the around-vertex step three times returns to the start, the order-three
# cycle of the three faces sharing a dodecahedron vertex. This is the {3} of the cell {5, 3}, the vertex figure of the
# dodecahedron, proved as a constructed local patch over all three faces by `fold`.
rule three-faces-around-a-vertex
  seat f, like vertex-face
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call around-vertex
        call around-vertex
          call around-vertex
            read f
      read f
  fold f

# THE SIDES-PER-FACE COUNT IS FIVE: every face of the dodecahedron is a pentagon, the first entry of {5, 3, 4}.
rule cell-faces-are-pentagons
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call sides-per-face
      code 5
  calm hold

# THE FACES-PER-VERTEX COUNT IS THREE: three faces meet at each vertex, the second entry of {5, 3, 4}.
rule cell-has-three-faces-per-vertex
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call faces-per-vertex
      code 3
  calm hold
